[{:db/id 17592186054439 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413067 :question/problem "If $B=\\{1,2,2,3,4,5,5\\}$, then $|B|=${{field-1}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413066 :question/problem "If $A=\\{0,x,x\\}$, then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413063 :question/problem "If $T=\\{x,{x}^{2},{x}^{3},y,\\{x,{x}^{2}\\},\\{x,y,y\\}\\}$, then $|T|=$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\nx,\\,{x}^{2},\\,{x}^{3},\\,y,\\,\\{x,{x}^{2}\\},\\,\\{x,y,y\\}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\{x,{x}^{2}\\}$ and $\\{x,y,y\\}$ each count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $6$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186348380 :question/problem "If $Y=\\{1,2,\\{\\{2\\}\\},3,2,4,\\{\\{∅\\}\\},4\\}$, then $|Y|=$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $Y$ is the number of elements in $Y.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $Y:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,\\{\\{2\\}\\},\\,3,\\,4,\\,\\{\\{∅\\}\\}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\{\\{2\\}\\}$ and $\\{\\{∅\\}\\}$ each count as single elements of $Y.$\n\nSince $Y$ has $6$ elements, the cardinality is $|Y|=6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055076 :question/problem "If $S=\\{1,\\{2,3\\},4,\\{5,6\\}\\}$, then $|S|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $S$ is the number of elements in $S.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $S:$\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\{2,3\\},\\,4,\\,\\{5,6\\}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\{2,3\\}$ and $\\{5,6\\}$ each count as single elements of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ has $4$ elements, the cardinality is $|S|=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055084 :question/problem "If $T=\\{\\{\\},x,y,\\{v,x,\\{w\\}\\}\\},$ then $|T|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\},\\,x,\\,y,\\,\\{v,x,\\{w\\}\\},\n$$\n\nNote that $\\{v,x,\\{w\\}\\}$ and $\\{\\}$ count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413065 :question/problem "If $A=\\{-3\\,322,-3\\,311,-3\\,300,…,2\\,332\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-3\\,322,\\,-3\\,311,\\,-3\\,300,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =11 \\\\  & =-3\\,311-(-3\\,322) \\\\  & =-3\\,300-(-3\\,311) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-3\\,322\\,+(n-1)\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =-3\\,322+11n-11 \\\\  & =11n-3\\,333.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{11n-3\\,333:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11N-3\\,333 & =2\\,332 \\\\ 11N & =5\\,665 \\\\ N & =\\frac{5\\,665}{11} \\\\ N & =515\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{11n-3\\,333:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 515\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $515$ elements, we must have $|A|=515.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287697 :question/problem "If $A=\\{-615,-610,-605,…,12\\,425\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-615,\\,-610,\\,-605,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-610-(-615) \\\\  & =-605-(-610) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-615+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-615+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-620.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{5n-620:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5N-620 & =12\\,425 \\\\ 5N & =13\\,045 \\\\ N & =\\frac{13\\,045}{5} \\\\ N & =2\\,609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{5n-620:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 2\\,609\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $2\\,609$ elements, we must have $|A|=2\\,609.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287695 :question/problem "If $A=\\{-1\\,250,-1\\,235,-1\\,220,…,19\\,300\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-1\\,250,\\,-1\\,235,\\,-1\\,220,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =15 \\\\  & =-1\\,235-(-1\\,250) \\\\  & =-1\\,220-(-1\\,235) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-1\\,250\\,+(n-1)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-1\\,250+15n-15 \\\\  & =15n-1\\,265.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15N-1\\,265 & =19\\,300 \\\\ 15N & =20\\,565 \\\\ N & =\\frac{20\\,565}{15} \\\\ N & =1\\,371\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 1\\,371\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $1\\,371$ elements, we must have $|A|=1\\,371.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055075 :question/problem "If $A=\\{1,4,7,…,295\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,4,\\,7,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =7-4 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{3n-2:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3N-2 & =295 \\\\ 3N & =297 \\\\ N & =\\frac{297}{3} \\\\ N & =99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{3n-2:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 99\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $99$ elements, we must have $|A|=99.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287693 :question/problem "If $A=\\{2,10,18,…,100410\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,18,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =8 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2+8n-8 \\\\  & =8n-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8N-6 & =100\\,410 \\\\ 8N & =100\\,416 \\\\ N & =\\frac{100\\,416}{8} \\\\ N & =12\\,552\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 12\\,552\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12\\,552$ elements, we must have $|A|=12\\,552.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413064 :question/problem "If $A=\\{5,20,80,320,…,5\\,368\\,709\\,120\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n5,\\,20,\\,80,\\,320,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{20} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=5({4}^{n-1})=\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{N} & =5\\,368\\,709\\,120 \\\\ {4}^{N} & =4\\,294\\,967\\,296 \\\\ N & ={log}_{4}⁡(4\\,294\\,967\\,296) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(4\\,294\\,967\\,296)}{{log}_{10}⁡(4)} \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{5}{4}(4)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287699 :question/problem "If $A=\\{2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287700 :question/problem "If $A=\\{3,18,108,648,…,181\\,398\\,528\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,108,\\,648,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({6}^{n-1})=\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{N} & =181\\,398\\,528 \\\\ {6}^{N} & =362\\,797\\,056 \\\\ N & ={log}_{6}⁡(362\\,797\\,056) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(362\\,797\\,056)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287702 :question/problem "If $A=\\{1,11,121,1\\,331,…,214\\,358\\,881\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,11,\\,121,\\,1\\,331,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{11} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {11}^{n-1}={11}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{N-1} & =214\\,358\\,881 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{11}⁡(214\\,358\\,881) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(214\\,358\\,881)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\ N-1 & =8 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $9$ elements, we must have $|A|=9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413068 :question/problem "If $A=\\{1,5,25,125,…,30\\,517\\,578\\,125\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,5,\\,25,\\,125,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {5}^{n-1}={5}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{{5}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{N-1} & =30\\,517\\,578\\,125 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{5}⁡(30\\,517\\,578\\,125) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(30\\,517\\,578\\,125)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N-1 & =15 \\\\ N & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{{5}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $16$ elements, we must have $|A|=16.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
